. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 *
. 
.         * I run a linear OLS regression analysis with the share of the man's 
> leisure
.         * time of the total leisure time of the couple (share_h_hobbies) as d
> ependent
.         * variable and the total number of daughters as independent variable.
>  The 
.         * regression has individual fixed effects and standard errors cluster
> ed at
.         * the individual level. This means that it examines the within-person
>  change
.         * in the dep var from having an additional daughter versus not having
>  one 
.         * (and having a son instead, since the total number of kids is held c
> onstant).
.         * As mentioned in the PAP, there is one observation per heterosexual 
> couple
.         * who does have children at some point in their time on the panel.
.         * The controls are the ones listed in the PAP, with adjustments as 
.         * documented below.
.         xtset pid syear

Panel variable: pid (unbalanced)
 Time variable: syear, 2001 to 2019, but with gaps
         Delta: 1 unit

.         
.         * Check how much of leisure time in a couple was the man's for men wh
> o have 
.         * daughters at some point, but for them before they father daughters.
.         * This is needed to see if the effect of daughters is that they chang
> e the
.         * intra-couple leisure time distribution in the direction of more gen
> der 
.         * equality, by providing a baseline to compare with.
.         * I (quietly) run a regression first just to have access to the relev
> ant sample.
.         qui xtreg share_h_hobbies total_daughters i.total_kids age2_100 p_age
>  p_age2_100 ///
>         loghinc pgbilzeit p_pgbilzeit ft_employed p_ft_employed disabled p_di
> sabled ///
>         i.marital_stat east_germany i.syear ib3.hgnuts1 if sex==1, fe vce(clu
> ster pid)

.         su share_h_hobbies if pre_daughter==1 & sex==1 & e(sample)

